        {
          "id": "SP_FEDORELLA_MINIMA_SIL_N_1947",
          "name": "Fedorella minima Silén, 1947",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Fedorella",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "Colony oval to pear-shaped (Figs 6 A, C, 7 A), 1.00 – 1.74 mm long by 0.88 – 1.37 mm wide (L / W 1.00 – 1.76, N 15), likely to be supported above the substrate by rhizooids. Autozooids arranged in 5 – 6 whorls of up to 10 – 12 zooids each (the highest number of zooids usually at colony mid-length), distinct with deep grooves and a thin rim of smooth calcification, irregularly polygonal, pentagonal, hexagonal or rectangular, with undulate margins, almost equidimensional (mean L / W 1.01). Frontal shield imperforate and coarsely tubercular; tuberculation seemingly increasing with ontogeny (Fig. 7 A); tubercles in the first two generations of zooids concentrated along the proximal and lateral margins of the zooid leaving the centre completely smooth (Figs 6 D, 7 E, F); in some zooids two pairs of circular marginal areolar pores visible laterally, one pair proximally and the other pair distally, at about the same level as orifice mid-length (Fig. 6 D). Primary orifice cleithridiate, slightly longer than wide (mean L / W 1.1), a horseshoe-shaped anter separated from a widely arcuate sinus by two small pointed triangular condyles proximomedially directed (Figs 6 B, 7 B). Paired oral spine bases present distolaterally only on the first two generations of autozooids (Figs 6 D arrowed, 7 F). Smooth closure plates observed sealing the orifice of the zooids in the first whorls (Fig.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:1006979",
          "children": [],
          "uuid": "2448b6e4-5ea4-572-818-26f1c9fc0ad0b0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
